1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,439
You type a name, and the computer needs a number. Who tells it which number?

2
00:00:04,459 --> 00:00:08,231
Get that wrong, and the server is right there, but nothing can find it.

3
00:00:08,701 --> 00:00:11,497
In this video: configure hostname resolution.

4
00:00:16,017 --> 00:00:18,600
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:19,070 --> 00:00:20,970
Configure hostname resolution.

6
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:24,819
We'll see where a name gets looked up, add a name by hand,

7
00:00:24,839 --> 00:00:27,222
and point a connection at a D N S server.

8
00:00:34,742 --> 00:00:40,484
Resolution turns a name into an I P address. The system asks its sources in order.

9
00:00:40,504 --> 00:00:46,316
The file slash e t c slash hosts comes first: names you write yourself. Then D N S:

10
00:00:46,336 --> 00:00:49,549
a server on the network that knows many names.

11
00:00:57,019 --> 00:01:00,298
The order is written in the file nsswitch dot conf,

12
00:01:00,318 --> 00:01:02,562
on the line that starts with hosts.

13
00:01:02,582 --> 00:01:06,637
And the D N S server comes from the Network Manager connection,

14
00:01:06,657 --> 00:01:09,031
which writes it into resolv dot conf.

15
00:01:16,501 --> 00:01:21,940
Before the demo, silently, the lab removes any old web one line from the hosts file,

16
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:26,294
and any old connection named lab one. This step changes the system.

17
00:01:34,814 --> 00:01:40,353
First, where are names looked up? Grep caret hosts, on the file nsswitch dot conf.

18
00:01:40,373 --> 00:01:43,403
The caret means: lines that start with hosts.

19
00:01:43,423 --> 00:01:46,250
Files first, then D N S, then my hostname.

20
00:01:56,770 --> 00:01:59,808
The local file. Cat slash e t c slash hosts.

21
00:01:59,828 --> 00:02:03,144
Each line is an address, then one or more names.

22
00:02:03,164 --> 00:02:05,923
Lines starting with a hash are comments.

23
00:02:13,393 --> 00:02:19,101
Now ask: get ent hosts, web one. Get ent asks the system the same way programs do.

24
00:02:19,121 --> 00:02:21,267
No output: the name is unknown.

25
00:02:29,787 --> 00:02:33,650
Add a name. Echo prints a line, and the pipe sends it to tee.

26
00:02:33,670 --> 00:02:38,424
Tee dash a appends it to the file. Sudo, because only root can write there.

27
00:02:38,444 --> 00:02:41,479
The line: an address, a full name, a short name.

28
00:02:50,949 --> 00:02:54,278
Ask again. Now the name resolves, to the address we wrote.

29
00:03:00,798 --> 00:03:05,950
Now D N S. The file resolv dot conf says which server the system asks.

30
00:03:05,970 --> 00:03:11,786
Network Manager generates it. Here: one server, ten dot zero dot two dot three.

31
00:03:21,306 --> 00:03:26,707
To set a D N S server, we use a connection. A virtual one again, called lab one.

32
00:03:26,727 --> 00:03:29,689
Then an address for it. And now the setting:

33
00:03:29,709 --> 00:03:32,806
I P v four dot D N S, with the server address.

34
00:03:45,326 --> 00:03:50,170
Connection up applies it. And nmcli can show the saved D N S setting.

35
00:03:59,690 --> 00:04:03,126
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

36
00:04:04,646 --> 00:04:09,219
We reboot. The name still resolves, and the D N S setting is still saved.

37
00:04:09,239 --> 00:04:13,561
The hosts file is a plain file, and the connection setting is stored,

38
00:04:13,581 --> 00:04:14,567
so both survive.

39
00:04:23,087 --> 00:04:28,374
Your turn. Make the name d b one resolve to ten dot ninety nine dot zero dot five,

40
00:04:28,394 --> 00:04:29,733
using the hosts file.

41
00:04:29,753 --> 00:04:35,040
Then create a connection called exam one, on a dummy interface called dummy three,

42
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:39,764
with the address ten dot ninety nine dot zero dot ten, slash twenty four,

43
00:04:39,784 --> 00:04:44,035
and the D N S server ten dot ninety nine dot zero dot fifty three.

44
00:04:44,055 --> 00:04:46,171
Activate it, and run the checker.

45
00:04:55,641 --> 00:04:57,392
Try before you open the solution.

46
00:05:02,912 --> 00:05:06,967
Next up: configure network services to start automatically at boot.

47
00:05:07,437 --> 00:05:10,339
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
